1. Introduction
1.1 Purpose
The purpose of this project is to ingest, inspect, clean, and perform descriptive exploratory data analysis (EDA) on a raw movies dataset (movies.csv). The workflow prepares raw tabular movie data for downstream analytics by identifying missing values, removing low-variance columns, verifying duplicates, optimizing column data types, and exporting a cleaned CSV file.
1.2 Scope
The system processes a compressed dataset (movies.csv.zip) containing 4,803 movie records across 24 attributes (including budget, genres, revenue, runtime, vote_average, cast, crew, and director). The final deliverables include a cleaned dataset (new_movies_clean.csv) and statistical insights covering movie budgets, revenues, genres, dominant languages, and directors.
1.3 Definitions & Abbreviations
•	CSV: Comma-Separated Values file format used for storing tabular data.
•	EDA: Exploratory Data Analysis — inspecting and summarizing dataset characteristics.
•	DataFrame (df): Two-dimensional labeled tabular data structure provided by the pandas library.
•	Null / NaN: Missing or undefined data values within dataset fields.
2. Overall Description
2.1 Product Perspective
This project is a standalone data analytics workflow implemented as a Python notebook in Google Colaboratory. It operates directly on uploaded CSV files using standard Python data science libraries without requiring an external database server or web frontend.
2.2 Hardware & Software Requirements
•	Execution Environment: Google Colaboratory (cloud-hosted Jupyter Notebook runtime).
•	Programming Language: Python 3.
•	pandas: Data loading, inspection, cleaning, transformation, and statistical analysis.
•	numpy: Numerical array operations and numeric type support.
•	zipfile: Extracting movies.csv directly from the uploaded movies.csv.zip archive.
